<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.virtualization</key>
    <true/>

    <!-- The app runs with the hardened runtime, which turns on library
         validation: a loaded dylib must be signed by the same Team ID as the
         process. libjcross_engine_glm.dylib is built by Cargo and embedded
         ad-hoc, so it has no Team ID and never matches — dyld refuses it with
         "different Team IDs" and the app dies at launch before any UI.

         The failure is worth naming because it reads as something else: the
         first line dyld prints is "Library not loaded", which points at a
         missing file, and the actual cause is three lines down in the list of
         paths it tried.

         The alternative — dropping the hardened runtime so validation stops
         applying — is what an ad-hoc `codesign --deep` re-sign does, and it
         silently gives up notarisation and every other hardened-runtime
         protection to fix one dylib. This entitlement gives up only library
         validation, which is the part actually in the way. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
